        "title": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d256b2ae-1787-5d30-bf56-d5f26293058d",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\sin x}{2x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\sin x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (2 x)-(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2 x)^{\\prime}}{(2 x)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\cos x\\cdot (2x)-\\sin x\\cdot 2}{{(2x)}^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{2(xcosx-\\sin x)}{4{x}^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{xcosx-\\sin x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\\prime}(\\pi )&=\\frac{\\pi \\cos (\\pi )-\\sin (\\pi )}{2(\\pi )^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi (-1)-(0)}{2{\\pi }^{2}}\\\\ &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2{\\pi }^{2}}\\\\ &=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.$",
